A speculative decode row is scheduled at its full width or not in this step.

The running-request loop trims a request's tokens to what the step budget
leaves: min(num_new_tokens, token_budget, input_budget - draft_slots). For a
row that carries draft tokens the trimmed step verifies fewer than
1 + num_spec tokens. The GDN kernels locate a request's recurrent state by the
accepted-token count of the previous step (num_accepted - 1 against the
conv-state row in causal_conv1d.py and against the block-table columns in
fused_recurrent.py / fused_sigmoid_gating.py); that arithmetic holds when
every verified step carried 1 + num_spec tokens. After a trimmed step the
count of the previous step can exceed the width of the current one: the
PR-50021 masks then read column 0 (zero output, no state stored) and the
conv-state shift reads past the row inside the tensor. The scheduler's own
comment on the async max_tokens check states the intent: "We don't schedule
partial draft tokens since this prevents uniform decode optimizations."

The trim occurs when a prefill chunk scheduled earlier in the same step
leaves 2..num_spec tokens of budget (a last chunk of 2041-2046 tokens at
max_num_batched_tokens 2048): the decode row that follows in `running` is cut
to that leftover. With the guard the row is skipped for the step, as it is at
budget 0, and runs at full width in the next one. The max_model_len bound
that follows still trims, because the request ends within those tokens.

Cut against the tree the whole series leaves (scheduler.py is last touched by
engine-completion-log). Replaces gdn-active-runtime-k-width, whose narrowing
of the state-index tensors to the step's width turned the trimmed step into
zeroed GDN output (and, on the full-CUDA-graph path, into a non-contiguous
state_indices view the fused MTP op rejects); with every spec step at full
width the num_spec + 1 columns are the layout the kernels index.

